{
  "id": "80593",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Relative Strengths of Acids and Bases",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ufspde7x2m6sqkx",
                "text": "Relative Strengths of Acids"
              }
            ],
            "id": "fs7kp7tj18393pn",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "All acids donate protons and form hydronium ions in aqueous solution, but specific acids differ significantly in how readily they do so. We can rank the strengths of acids by the extent to which they ionize in aqueous solution. Using HA to represent a generic acid, the reaction of an acid with water is given by the expression:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "zkvz52ojhin7uon",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "qotwjia3f6w6ytu",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "w36lca5qi41qadf",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{HA}(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm A^-(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Water is the base that reacts with the acid HA, A"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the conjugate base of the acid HA, and the hydronium ion (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is the conjugate acid of water. A "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "strong acid"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An acid that completely dissociates or ionizes in water to produce hydronium ion."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3727558620",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1658049672",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " yields 100% (or very nearly so) of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and A"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " when the acid ionizes in water. Hydrochloric acid (HCl), nitric acid (HNO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "), and sulfuric acid (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ") are the most common strong acids."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3q6rczldqatshar",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "weak acid"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An acid that only partially dissociates or ionizes in water to produce hydronium ion."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3788914390",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "448447130",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " ionizes only to a small extent, producing small amounts of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and A"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " at equilibrium. The relative strengths of acids may be determined by measuring their equilibrium constants in aqueous solutions. In solutions of the same concentration, stronger acids ionize to a greater extent and yield higher concentrations of hydronium ions than do weaker acids. We can write the equation for the ionization of the weak acid HA representing the hydrated proton as either hydronium ion (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ") or hydrogen ion (H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "):"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ph5l44apa8bjl11",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ielkrk0w9cihwrj",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "utdc6x4i23d9sxt",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{HA}(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm A^-(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "hpb86u2ibzf49r5",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "spkqotjj10op0tz",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{or}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{HA}(aq)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm H^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm A^-(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "and we can write the equilibrium constant expression as either:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "drjphjtxglb29l3",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "p2kmoe61ywdagl2",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "yzqsu2f942u9mq3",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "K_{\\mathrm a}=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm A^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HA}\\rbrack}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; \\mathrm{or}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_{\\mathrm a}=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm H^+\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm A^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HA}\\rbrack}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "where the concentrations are those at equilibrium. (Although water is a reactant, its concentration is essentially constant, and we do not include [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O] in the equilibrium constant expression.) The equilibrium constant for the ionization of an acid, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "a"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is called the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "acid-ionization constant"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The equilibrium constant for an acid-ionization reaction; also called "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "acid-dissociation constant"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1132003980",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3212816370",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", or acid-dissociation constant. The larger the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "a"
              },
              {
                "text": " of an acid, the higher the concentrations of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and A"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " relative to the concentration of the nonionized acid, HA. The acid-ionization constants increase as the strengths of the acids increase and decrease as their strengths decrease."
              }
            ],
            "id": "mh0y0dlgve9nfyu",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "372kdvs677ecldu",
                "text": "Relative Strengths of Bases"
              }
            ],
            "id": "nuh0nk9kt6f4ax0",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "All bases accept protons and form hydroxide ions in aqueous solution, but specific bases differ significantly in the extent to which they do so. We can rank the strengths of bases by their tendency to form hydroxide ions in aqueous solution. A "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "strong base"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A base that completely dissociates or ionizes in water to produce hydroxide ion."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1101688097",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1639740050",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " yields 100% (or very nearly so) of OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " and M"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " when it reacts with water. Most strong bases are soluble hydroxides of group 1 or group 2 elements. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "j6050b6t279kv8i",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "weak base"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A base that only partially dissociates or ionizes in water to produce hydroxide ion."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2993644804",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "915099685",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " yields only a small proportion of hydroxide ions at equilibrium. The relative strengths of bases may also be determined by measuring their equilibrium constants in aqueous solutions. For the reaction of a base with water:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "kyzhxhgz92hlw0x",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "y87sd1ehqef5l9v",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "z0wp5hxtzwxcf6l",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm B(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm{HB}^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "we write the equilibrium constant as:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "a24jxv72vgblkmr",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "o4ztgunfgd48vl4",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "t65ppq03aex2wcm",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "K_{\\mathrm b}\\;=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HB}^+\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm{OH}^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack\\mathrm B\\rbrack}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "B is the base, water is the acid that reacts with B, HB"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the conjugate acid of B, the hydroxide ion is the conjugate base of water, and the concentrations are those at equilibrium. (Again, we do not include [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O] in the equation because its concentration is essentially constant.) The equilibrium constant for the ionization of a base, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "b"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is called the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "base-ionization constant"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The equilibrium constant for a base-ionization reaction; also called "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "base-dissociation constant"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1098973728",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1732526187",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", or base-dissociation constant. The larger the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "b"
              },
              {
                "text": " of a base, the higher the concentrations of OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " and HB"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " relative to the concentration of the nonionized base, B. The base-ionization constants increase as the strengths of the bases increase and decrease as their strengths decrease."
              }
            ],
            "id": "jdtqm19491iejdh",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
